import Foundation

/// DRAFT CONTENT: pending medical and editorial review.
/// Lessons for days 1 to 30, Build the Foundation.
extension ProgrammeLessonsDraft {

    static let phaseOne: [ProgrammeLesson?] = [

        // MARK: Week 1, Start & Baseline

        make(1, health: false,
             "The programme runs for 90 days, grouped into 13 weeks and three phases. Days 1 to 30 build the foundation, days 31 to 60 help you discover your patterns, and days 61 to 90 are about personalising what you have learned and keeping it going.",
             "Every day has the same simple shape: today's focus, a short read like this one, a few actions that open the trackers you already have in Genesyx, and a line on why the day matters. Most days take a few minutes.",
             "The programme counts calendar days from the day you started, so it keeps moving whether or not you open the app. A day without a log never resets anything and never sends you back to day 1. Whenever you return, you simply continue with today.",
             "Your kit and the programme work together, but neither rushes the other. Later weeks explain each item in the box when it becomes relevant."),
        make(2,
             "Genesyx needs one date from you before it can show where you are in your cycle: the first day of your last period. If you entered it when you set up the app, check it is still right. If a period has started since, update it.",
             "Your cycle length and period length are settings too. If you are not sure of them, the starting values are a reasonable place to begin and you can correct them later. Nothing is fixed.",
             "Then log once. Open the Daily Log and tap a mood, an energy level, or anything else you notice. Everything in the log is optional, and a short entry counts just as much as a full one.",
             "That is today done. Tomorrow looks at the Daily Log in a little more detail."),
        make(3,
             "The Daily Log is a short record of how a day went. It holds a mood, an energy level, any symptoms you noticed, and a note if something was different. Sleep, water and supplements each open their own small sheet from the same place.",
             "Nothing in it is required. Tap what you notice and skip what you do not. An entry that says very little is still an entry, and an ordinary day is as worth recording as an unusual one.",
             "You can also add or correct an earlier day from the calendar, so a day you did not log at the time is not lost. Days in the future cannot be logged.",
             "The programme's daily actions open this same log. There is no second tracker to learn: what you record here is what the programme reads."),
        make(4,
             "Genesyx records food by group and not by item. There are six groups: vegetables, fruit, starchy carbs, protein, dairy and alternatives, and oils and fats. When a meal includes something from a group, you tick that group for the day.",
             "This is a record, not a target. Nothing here is scored, and a day with few ticks or none costs you nothing. The record simply shows which groups appeared across your day.",
             "For this first week, eat as you normally do and tick what applies. Later weeks each bring one small nutrition focus, and they make more sense when you can see your usual pattern first."),
        make(5,
             "Your kit includes ovulation tests, vaginal pH test strips and pregnancy tests, along with a printed Programme Guide and your access card. Each test comes with its own instructions leaflet, and that leaflet is always the place to check how to use it and how to read it.",
             "Ovulation tests look for LH in urine. The programme covers them in week 6, and again in week 7 alongside the other tests.",
             "A pH strip gives a number. Genesyx keeps those numbers as a history so you can see your own pattern over time. Week 7 looks at this in detail, and one reading a week is plenty.",
             "For a pregnancy test, the result is yours to read using the instructions that came with it. Where Genesyx offers a place to record what the strip shows, it keeps that record and nothing more.",
             "Until those weeks arrive, the tests can stay in the box."),
        make(6,
             "A baseline is simply a description of how things are now. This week you may have logged a mood, some sleep, some water or a few food groups. Whatever is there, even one entry, is your starting point.",
             "Nothing about a baseline is a judgement. It is not a score to improve on, and there is no level you are meant to have reached. It exists so that, later in the programme, you can compare like with like instead of relying on memory.",
             "If you would like to, write a short note in today's log about how this week felt. A sentence is enough."),
        make(7,
             "Each programme week ends with a review. It gathers what you recorded over the seven days: your check-ins, the lessons you opened and the routines that showed up most often.",
             "The review reports what is there. It does not grade the week, and a quiet week is shown as it is, without comment. Where there is too little recorded to say anything, it says so plainly instead of guessing.",
             "Your consistency figure counts only the actions that were relevant to you on each day. It measures whether you did the actions, never what your body did.",
             "Next week is about your cycle: how it is counted, and the difference between what you record and what Genesyx estimates."),

        // MARK: Week 2, Understand Your Cycle

        make(8,
             "Cycle day 1 is the first day of your period. The count carries on until the day before your next period starts, and that total is your cycle length.",
             "Genesyx describes four phases. Period runs from day 1 for as many days as your period lasts. Follicular runs from the end of your period up to the day before predicted ovulation. Ovulatory is the single predicted ovulation day. Luteal runs from the day after that until your next period starts.",
             "The phase names are a way of describing where you are in the count. The boundaries between them are worked out from the dates and lengths you have entered, so they are estimates and not measurements of what your body is doing on a given day.",
             "The guide on how your cycle and phases work goes through the set-up screen and where each phase appears in the app."),
        make(9,
             "Open your cycle settings and look at three things: the first day of your last period, your cycle length and your period length. If any of them no longer matches what happened, change it. Corrections are a normal part of using the app.",
             "When your next period starts, record that day. If it arrives earlier or later than Genesyx estimated, nothing has gone wrong: the estimate was an estimate, and your record is what counts.",
             "If you are unsure of your usual cycle length, leave the current setting and let a few recorded cycles show you. A length based on what you have recorded describes you better than a figure from memory.",
             "Today's other actions are your everyday check-ins in the Daily Log."),
        make(10,
             "The calendar tints each day by what it is: period, fertile window, ovulation, luteal or follicular. A legend underneath names the colours, and a card repeats your current phase in words.",
             "Days you have logged carry their own marker, so you can see at a glance where your record is and where it has gaps. Tapping a day opens it, and you can add or edit a log for any day up to today.",
             "Future days are always estimates. They move if you change your settings or record a period on a different day from the one predicted. Read them as a likely range drawn from your own dates.",
             "As you record each period, your cycle history grows from that point on, where your app shows it. Genesyx does not fill in cycles from before you started recording."),
        make(11,
             "Variety here means something simple: which of the six food groups turn up across your days. Open Your Diet Plan and look at the last week. Some groups may be there most days, and others only now and then.",
             "This is a record, not a target, and nothing here is scored. A group that appears rarely is just a fact about your week. It is not a gap you are being asked to close.",
             "If you notice something that interests you, hold on to it. Later weeks look at single groups one at a time, and what you have noticed will make those weeks more useful.",
             "Then tick today's groups as usual."),
        make(12,
             "A recorded date is something that happened and that you logged: the day a period started, for example. An estimated date is arithmetic. Genesyx takes your last period start and your cycle length and works out when the next period, the fertile window and ovulation are likely to fall.",
             "Estimates are useful for planning and they are often close. They are still predictions. Your body does not read the calendar, and a predicted date arriving does not mean the event has happened.",
             "Cycle length varies from person to person and from one cycle to the next. Travel, illness, a demanding few weeks or a change in sleep can all move things by days. A cycle that is a little longer or shorter than the last one is common.",
             "If your cycles are very irregular, or something changes and stays changed, a GP or nurse is the right person to ask."),
        make(13,
             "This week covered a lot of vocabulary: cycle day, cycle length, phases, recorded dates and estimated dates. You do not need to hold all of it in your head. The calendar keeps the dates, and the words are there when you want them.",
             "What is worth keeping is the one distinction underneath it all. Some things on your screen happened and were logged by you. Others are Genesyx's estimate from your settings. Both are useful, and they answer different questions.",
             "If you have a note to add about this cycle so far, today's log is a good place for it."),
        make(14,
             "This week's review may include cycle entries alongside your everyday check-ins. If you recorded a period start or corrected a date, it appears as something you tracked.",
             "Cycle and period actions are optional in the programme. They are offered when they are relevant, and they are never counted against you when there is nothing to record. A week in which no period started is simply a week in which no period started.",
             "Looking ahead, week 3 turns to food. You will meet Your Diet Plan properly and look at how the six food groups fit together, with no perfect plate required."),

        // MARK: Week 3, Nutrition Foundations

        make(15,
             "Your Diet Plan is not a diet in the usual sense. It does not set rules, count anything you eat or tell you to cut foods out. It is a place to record food by group and to see a little more about your own routine.",
             "It brings several things together: today's food record, the nutrition focus for the current programme week, a guide to each food group and some meal ideas that show which groups they include.",
             "The food record keeps its own principle: it is a record, not a target, and nothing here is scored. The programme notes whether you did a nutrition check-in. It never grades which groups you ate or how many.",
             "If you have a medical condition, an allergy or a diet set by a professional, their advice comes first."),
        make(16,
             "A nutrition check-in takes a few seconds. Think about what you ate, and tick each food group it included. A cheese sandwich with salad, for instance, covers starchy carbs, dairy and alternatives, and vegetables.",
             "You can tick as you go through the day or all at once in the evening. If you eat something that does not sit neatly in a group, leave it out or add your own label. Your own labels are kept for next time.",
             "The programme shows this action as completed once you have recorded something. The number of groups is shown as a plain count for your information and is never turned into a score or a percentage."),
        make(17,
             "The six groups are fixed, so your record stays comparable from week to week. Vegetables covers things like salad, greens, peppers and carrots. Fruit covers berries, apples, bananas and citrus.",
             "Starchy carbs covers potatoes, bread, rice, pasta and oats. Protein covers beans, pulses, fish, eggs, meat and tofu. Dairy and alternatives covers milk, yoghurt, cheese and fortified alternatives. Oils and fats covers olive oil, nuts, seeds and avocado.",
             "These lists are examples to help you place a meal. They are not a list of what to eat, and no group is ranked above another.",
             "Many meals touch several groups at once. Tick each one that applies and move on."),
        make(18,
             "A balanced plate is a simple idea: a meal that draws on several food groups, not just one. Many everyday meals already do this without any planning. A stir-fry with rice, vegetables and tofu covers three groups before you count the oil it was cooked in.",
             "Choose one meal today and name the groups in it. That is the whole exercise. If the meal covers one or two groups, that is a fact about that meal and nothing more.",
             "Balance is something that shows across days and weeks, not something each plate has to achieve. Meal ideas in Your Diet Plan list the groups each one includes, if you would like some inspiration."),
        make(19,
             "There is no perfect way to eat, and Genesyx does not pretend otherwise. Your food record does not label foods, rank your days or hold you to a number of groups. A day with one tick sits in your history exactly as a day with six does.",
             "This is deliberate. A food log is easy to turn into a scoreboard, and a scoreboard tends to make people log what looks right instead of what happened. An honest record is the useful one.",
             "The programme will suggest a small nutrition focus each week, such as noticing variety or trying a different protein food. These are invitations. Take the ones that suit you and leave the rest.",
             "For advice about your own diet, a GP, pharmacist or registered dietitian can help."),
        make(20,
             "Routines around food are shaped by time, money, work, family and energy as much as by choice. Looking at them kindly is more useful than looking at them critically.",
             "Think about a typical week. Some meals are probably reliable and need little thought. Others may be rushed, skipped or different every day. Neither kind is a problem. They are just the shape of your week.",
             "You do not need to change anything as a result. If one small adjustment comes to mind that would make your week easier, note it in today's log and come back to it when the programme reaches practical preparation in week 10."),
        make(21,
             "In your review, nutrition appears in two plain lines: whether a nutrition check-in was completed, and how many of the six food groups were recorded. There is no nutrition percentage and no rating.",
             "The nutrition check-in is offered every day and is never counted against you. If you skipped it on some days, your review simply shows the days you did record.",
             "Next week moves to two everyday habits: water and sleep. Both are tracked simply in Genesyx, and the week is about routines you can keep, not about hitting a figure every day."),

        // MARK: Week 4, Hydration & Sleep

        make(22,
             "The easiest way to build a water habit is to anchor it to moments that already happen: a glass with each meal, one when you sit down to work, one when you get home. The decision is made in advance, so there is nothing to remember.",
             "Genesyx shows a daily water target and lets you log each glass with a tap. The target is a guide for tracking. How much anyone needs varies with heat, activity and health, and for most healthy adults thirst is a reasonable guide.",
             "Logging water is about noticing your routine, not about reaching a perfect total every day. A day where you logged three glasses is a useful entry.",
             "If you are unusually thirsty for a long time, or have been given advice about fluids by a clinician, speak to a GP or pharmacist."),
        make(23,
             "Water first. Each time you finish a glass, tap to add it. If you only remember in the evening, add what you can recall. An approximate total is more useful than none.",
             "Then sleep. Genesyx asks for one number: how long you slept. You enter it yourself in hours and minutes. Nothing is measured by your phone, and there is no need to be exact to the minute.",
             "Neither entry is judged. A short night is recorded as a short night, and the programme looks only at whether you logged your sleep, never at how long it was. Water builds towards your daily target a glass at a time, and part of the way there still counts."),
        make(24,
             "The hydration tracker shows how much water you have logged today against your daily target, with a progress bar and buttons to add or remove a glass. If you over-count, take one off.",
             "A short line of guidance sits with it and changes through the day, suggesting a moment to drink that fits the hour. It is there to save you a decision.",
             "In the programme, water is the one check-in that builds gradually. Each glass you log moves today's action forward, so part of your target still counts for something. Other check-ins are simply done or not yet done.",
             "Your target is a tracking aid. It is fine to end a day below it."),
        make(25,
             "Meals are the most dependable anchors most people have. They happen at roughly the same times, you are usually sitting down, and a drink is already part of the setting. Adding a glass of water to each one is a small step.",
             "Food contributes to your fluids as well. Fruit, vegetables, soup and yoghurt all contain water, and tea and coffee count too. The water tracker only counts what you log, so it will always be a partial picture, and that is fine.",
             "Log each glass when you have it. If today goes differently, log what you did drink. This is a habit you are trying out, not a rule."),
        make(26,
             "Sleep in Genesyx is a duration you enter yourself, in hours and minutes. The app does not use your phone's sensors or a watch, and it does not rate the quality of your sleep or track when you went to bed.",
             "Enter last night roughly, first thing if you can. Do not worry about fifteen minutes either way. Across a week, approximate entries add up to a readable picture: your average, and how many nights you logged.",
             "Nights you did not log are left out of the average instead of being counted as zero, so gaps do not distort it.",
             "The programme only looks at whether you logged your sleep. A short night is never marked down. If poor sleep is a lasting problem for you, a GP or pharmacist can talk it through."),
        make(27,
             "Most people have more routine than they realise. There is usually a fixed point in the morning, such as making a drink or leaving the house, and another in the evening, such as plugging in a phone or locking the door.",
             "These fixed points are useful. A new habit attached to one of them tends to hold, because the old habit acts as the reminder. Logging last night's sleep while the kettle boils is an example.",
             "Think about your own fixed points today. If one of them looks like a natural home for a check-in, try it there for the rest of the week and see whether it holds."),
        make(28,
             "This week's review draws on the same weekly summary that the Insights screen uses. For sleep it shows your average over the nights you logged and the number of nights recorded. For water it shows the days you logged.",
             "Read both as descriptions of your week. A lower average or fewer logged days is information, not a mark against you, and the review will not present it as one.",
             "You are now four weeks in. The trackers you have met so far, the Daily Log, the cycle calendar, Your Diet Plan, water and sleep, are the core of the programme. The coming weeks add understanding on top of them."),

        // MARK: Week 5, Your Body Signals (first two days)

        make(29,
             "Mood and energy are the two quickest entries in the Daily Log. Mood offers four choices, from Great to Low. Energy offers three: Low, Normal or High. You pick the one that fits and move on.",
             "There is no need to explain or justify an entry. A low day is logged as a low day. The programme counts the check-in as done whatever you chose, and a low mood or low energy never lowers your consistency.",
             "The value of these entries comes later. Memory tends to recolour a whole week with how you feel today. A row of small entries made at the time is more reliable.",
             "If low mood is persistent or is getting in the way of daily life, please speak to a GP."),
        make(30,
             "Thirty days ago you had not logged anything in this programme. Today there is a record: days with activity, check-ins completed and lessons read. Open your programme progress to see it.",
             "The figures describe what you did, and they stop at the end of yesterday so that today is never shown as incomplete. Activities that were optional or not relevant to you were never part of the count.",
             "If there are stretches with little recorded, they are part of the picture and nothing more. The programme did not reset during them, and it does not ask you to make them up.",
             "The second phase, Discover Your Patterns, runs from day 31 to day 60. It covers fertility and testing, and then starts to look at what your record can show."),
    ]
}
